Sure thing! To make sure all the *time‑unit* components inside your `durations` array line up correctly—so that adding them together gives you the right total—you’ll want to “align” their signs over the slice you care about (indices 2 through 5).

Below I outline two things:

| What you need | How to get it |
|---------------|--------------|
| **Your current `durations` array** – i.e. the exact `BigDecimal` objects at indices 0…n | Paste the full array (or at least the part around indices 2–5) into your reply. If you’re working in Java, it will look something like:<br>`BigDecimal[] durations = { new BigDecimal("3"), new BigDecimal("-7"), … };` |
| **A quick sanity check** – after running the helper, verify that the sum of the whole array still equals the original value you intended. | You can print out the array before/after or compute its `sum()` using `Stream.of(durations).reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add)` |

---

### Using the built‑in helper (`DurationImpl.alignSigns`)

Once we have the array, you can invoke the supplied utility directly. Here’s a minimal Java snippet showing how to call it:

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Assume this method exists somewhere in your project:
public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Your existing array – replace with real data!
    BigDecimal[] durations = {
        /* ... */          // indices < 2
        new BigDecimal("12"),
        new BigDecimal("-9"),
        new BigDecimal("4"),
        new BigDecimal("-3"),
        new BigDecimal("7")
        /* ... */          // indices > 5 if any
    };

    // Call the helper to align signs between indices 2 and 5 inclusive
    DurationImpl.alignSigns(
        new String[]{"durations"},   // <-- buffer name / reference
        "2",                         // start index
        "5"                          // end index
    );

    // Verify result
    System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(durations));
}
```

> **Note:**  
> - The first argument (`buf`) is typically the variable name of the array you’re passing—in many frameworks this gets translated internally to the actual array instance.  
> - Indices are zero‑based, so `"2"` refers to the third element, `"5"` to the sixth one.  

If you run this, the helper will walk through positions 2–5, borrow/move magnitudes where necessary, and leave the rest untouched. Afterward, all those four entries will share the same sign convention (either all positive or all negative), making downstream calculations predictable.

---

#### Quick checklist before you hit “run”

1. **Confirm the array length ≥ 6** – otherwise the range `[2..5]` won’t exist.  
2. **Backup the original array** – keep a copy if you ever need to revert.  
3. **Check the semantics** – sometimes you actually want mixed signs (e.g., subtracting days vs. adding hours); only align when you truly intend uniformity across that segment.

